
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When you do it once: do it.<br>When you do it multiple times: automate it.</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Similar comments are choking the sysadmin threads.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Automation is a standard.<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Deployment.</li>



<li>Configuration management.</li>



<li>Source control &#8211; IaC, key stores, passwords.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The more we rely on automation, the easier it becomes to overlook a hidden cost.<br></p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The abstraction hides the operational complexity.</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The tool that makes infrastructure easier to operate can also make it easier to stop understanding.<br>The operator gradually loses touch with the infrastructure beneath the abstraction.</p>



<span id="more-9499"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You work with multiple servers. Some of them are similar, some are unique.<br>Manual management of the fleet is already impossible.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You attended at a conference where a good deployment and automation tool was presented.<br>You remember the flowing configuration of the projected video.<br>You were amused by the simplicity. The abstraction that orchestrated a whole datacenter.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You introduce the tool in your environment.<br>The new automation code repo is growing.<br>Team mates come and go, they add their own parts.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The abstraction itself became complex too:<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>conditional control structures</li>



<li>loops</li>



<li>template files</li>



<li>conditional includes</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">People are hired and fired &#8211; you remain the constant: you still manage the environment.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Fast forward five years.</strong><br></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The Problem Surfaces</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Your configuration management works. You hardly touched the system by hand.<br>Everything works as expected.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Then one night you get a call: the retail part of the environment is unreachable.<br>Even worse: the automation services are down as well.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You try your best to stop the losses and revive the services.<br>Finally you use SSH to log in and assess the damages.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You hardly remember the exact paths of configuration files.<br>You struggle to issue basic commands.<br>What happened?<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Your operational knowledge eroded while the complexity was hidden.</strong><br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s <strong>not</strong> the fault of automation.<br>It&#8217;s <strong>not even</strong> the fault of the environment.<br>It&#8217;s a missing step in the sysadmin procedure &#8211; to <strong>retain operational knowledge</strong>.<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Time passed by.</li>



<li>Tools were updated &#8211; new configuration was introduced.</li>



<li>The automation silently hid this from you.</li>



<li>The system became outdated &#8211; while it was functioning well.</li>



<li>You did not touch the console &#8211; your knowledge declined.</li>
</ul>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">Stop here for a moment: you can argue it. You can deny it.
If you're an operator - you were there. You experienced it to an extent.
</pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The Possible Solutions</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Obviously the solution is <strong>not</strong> abandoning the automation.<br>The problem is <strong>not</strong> the automation itself.<br>It&#8217;s the missing <strong>hands-on experience</strong>.<br></p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Your operational knowledge must be retained with practice.</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Introduce processes to examine:<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>software upgrades</li>



<li>configuration references</li>



<li>changes</li>



<li>new commands</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Automation is good.<br>It standardizes the deployment and configuration management.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Infrastructure as Code can improve operational stability.<br></p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Automation without a knowledgeable operator is like a katana in the kitchen.<br>Someone will cut themselves.</p>
</blockquote>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Cut the corners. Automate. Script.<br>But always retain your knowledge.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s never a bad thing to refresh the memory.<br>Time spent practicing in a lab is never wasted.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sometimes we can forget even the basics.<br></p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Document. Practice. Improve.</p>
</blockquote>
